@startuml
!include style.puml
title QPitch 2.0 — audio and pitch-control paths
start
:Host calls processBlock();
if (Empty block or no outputs?) then (yes)
  stop
endif
:Read parameters and allowed-note mask;
:Copy input channels to dryBuffer;
fork
  :Detect pitch from input channel 0\n(YIN and confidence);
  :Convert Hz to MIDI; choose allowed target\nwith note locking and transition smoothing;
  :Apply tolerance, correction amount, retune speed,\nsnappiness, T-Pain and humanize;
  :Calculate pitch ratio\n(or hold / relax after loss of pitch);
fork again
  :Retain original input for delayed dry path;
end fork
:Feed all input channels to one PitchShifter\nwith ratio = 1 when correction is inactive;
:Adapt host blocks to fixed Rubber Band blocks;
:Shift channels together\nwith selected native formant mode;
:Crossfade shifted audio and latency-matched dry\n(5 ms fade; wet target = 1 or 0);
note right
  Correction inactive: host bypass, correction off,
  zero correction amount, or no allowed notes.
  The engine keeps running in either mode.
end note
:Duplicate mono output for mono-to-stereo layout;
:Detect measured output pitch from channel 0;
:Publish input, target and output pitch readouts;
:Apply output gain;
:Return audio to host;
stop
legend bottom
  This is the order within one host audio callback.
  The fork separates logical control and audio paths;
  it does not imply worker threads.
  prepareToPlay() reports adapter + engine latency to the host.
endlegend
@enduml
